    "title_eng": "2.4 Power Governance Subsystems: WakeLocks, Runtime PM, and Energy-Aware Scheduling",
    "content_eng": "# 2.4 Power Management Subsystem: WakeLocks, Runtime PM & Energy-Aware Scheduling\n### Android Systems & Bionic Architecture Handbook — Module 2: Lecture 4\n\n**Course Series:** AOSF-HB-2026-SYS-MOD2-04  \n**Author:** Eunho Kim (@uno-km), AMEVA Systems Architecture & Research Engineering Group  \n**Target Architecture:** ARM64 Heterogeneous DynamIQ Multi-Core (Cortex-X / A700 / A500)  \n**Curriculum Scope:** Kernel WakeLocks, Suspend-to-RAM, Energy-Aware Scheduling (EAS), Thermal Throttling  \n**Compliance Standard:** Apache-2.0 / OpenSSF Best Practices / CNCF Neutrality Guidelines  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Abstract & The Mobile Power Frontier\n\nIn untethered mobile devices, computational performance is strictly bounded by thermal dissipation and battery chemistry. Unlike desktops with active cooling fans and wall power, smartphones cannot sustain maximum clock frequencies without triggering severe skin thermal safety limits (40°C~45°C) or exhausting batteries within hours.\n\nAndroid addresses these thermodynamic boundaries through three cooperative kernel subsystems:\n1. **WakeLocks & Opportunistic Suspend**: Enforcing hardware Deep Sleep (Suspend-to-RAM) whenever user interaction ceases.\n2. **Energy-Aware Scheduling (EAS)**: Unifying CPU frequency scaling (schedutil governor) and task placement using an exact architectural Energy Model.\n3. **Dynamic Thermal Management (DTM)**: Real-time closed-loop closed-loop thermal throttling protecting silicon from electromigration.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive WASM Systems Engineering Lab (Colab-Style Cells)\n\n### [Cell 1~3] Python Energy-Aware Scheduling (EAS) Placement Simulator\n```python\n# [Colab Cell 1: Environment & EAS Setup]\nimport sys\nimport math\n\nprint(\"=== [Cell 1: Energy-Aware Scheduling Model Initialized] ===\")\nprint(\"Python Version:\", sys.version.split()[0])\n\n# [Colab Cell 2: EAS Energy Model Cost Function]\nclass EnergyAwareScheduler:\n    \"\"\"\n    Simulates the Linux Energy-Aware Scheduling (EAS) task placement algorithm.\n    Energy Cost E = sum(Active_Power(cluster, opp) * util + Idle_Power(cluster) * (1 - util))\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.clusters = {\n            \"LITTLE (Cortex-A510)\": {\"max_capacity\": 300, \"active_pwr_mw\": 85, \"idle_pwr_mw\": 5},\n            \"MID (Cortex-A715)\":    {\"max_capacity\": 750, \"active_pwr_mw\": 350, \"idle_pwr_mw\": 15},\n            \"PRIME (Cortex-X3)\":    {\"max_capacity\": 1024, \"active_pwr_mw\": 1250, \"idle_pwr_mw\": 40}\n        }\n\n    def select_optimal_cluster(self, task_utilization):\n        best_cluster = None\n        min_energy = float('inf')\n\n        for name, spec in self.clusters.items():\n            if task_utilization <= spec[\"max_capacity\"]:\n                norm_util = task_utilization / spec[\"max_capacity\"]\n                energy = (spec[\"active_pwr_mw\"] * norm_util) + (spec[\"idle_pwr_mw\"] * (1.0 - norm_util))\n                if energy < min_energy:\n                    min_energy = energy\n                    best_cluster = name\n        \n        # If task exceeds all, default to Prime\n        if not best_cluster:\n            best_cluster = \"PRIME (Cortex-X3)\"\n            min_energy = self.clusters[\"PRIME (Cortex-X3)\"][\"active_pwr_mw\"]\n\n        return best_cluster, round(min_energy, 2)\n\n# [Colab Cell 3: Verification & Execution Benchmark]\neas = EnergyAwareScheduler()\ntests = [(\"UI Animation Frame\", 150), (\"Audio Decoding Worker\", 80), (\"Heavy Tensor GEMM Pass\", 950)]\n\nprint(\"\\n[EAS Task Placement Decisions]\")\nfor task_name, util in tests:\n    cluster, pwr = eas.select_optimal_cluster(util)\n    print(f\"Task: {task_name:<24} (Util: {util:>4}) -> Placed on {cluster} (Power Cost: {pwr} mW)\")\n\nassert eas.select_optimal_cluster(80)[0] == \"LITTLE (Cortex-A510)\", \"Low util must land on LITTLE core\"\nprint(\"Status: 100% Deterministic EAS Placement PASS\")\n\n```\n\n### [Clang C99] CPU DVFS Operating Performance Point (OPP) Selector\n```c\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n#include <stdint.h>\n\n// DVFS Operating Performance Point (OPP) Table Selector\ntypedef struct {\n    uint32_t freq_khz;\n    uint32_t volt_uv;\n    uint32_t power_mw;\n} OppEntry;\n\nOppEntry opp_table[] = {\n    {  300000, 650000,  45 },\n    {  800000, 720000, 120 },\n    { 1400000, 810000, 310 },\n    { 2000000, 920000, 680 },\n    { 2800000, 1050000, 1450 }\n};\n\nint select_opp_by_util(uint32_t target_freq) {\n    int entries = sizeof(opp_table) / sizeof(opp_table[0]);\n    for (int i = 0; i < entries; i++) {\n        if (opp_table[i].freq_khz >= target_freq) {\n            return i;\n        }\n    }\n    return entries - 1; // Highest OPP\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"=== CPU DVFS Frequency Governor OPP Selection Simulator ===\\n\");\n    uint32_t demand_freq = 1250000; // 1.25 GHz demand\n    int idx = select_opp_by_util(demand_freq);\n\n    printf(\"  Demand Frequency : %u kHz\\n\", demand_freq);\n    printf(\"  Selected OPP     : %u kHz @ %u uV (%u mW)\\n\", \n           opp_table[idx].freq_khz, opp_table[idx].volt_uv, opp_table[idx].power_mw);\n    printf(\"  Status           : VALIDATED (Safe hardware operating point locked)\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}\n\n```\n\n### [POSIX Shell] Mobile Power Governance & WakeLock Inspector\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -eu\necho \"=== [Android Power Supply & WakeLock State Probe] ===\"\necho \"Timestamp: $(date -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S UTC')\"\n\necho -e \"\n[1. WakeLock Node Verification]\"\nif [ -f \"/sys/power/wake_lock\" ]; then\n    echo \"  Active User WakeLocks: $(cat /sys/power/wake_lock 2>/dev/null || echo 'None')\"\nelse\n    echo \"  [Sandbox Mode] Virtual WakeLock: PowerManagerService Acquired (SCREEN_DIM_WAKE_LOCK)\"\nfi\n\necho -e \"\n[2. CPU Governor State]\"\necho \"  Primary Governor: schedutil (EAS Energy Model Active)\"\necho \"  Energy Savings  : ~35% reduction vs performance governor\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Deep Architectural Deconstruction\n\n### 3.1 Kernel WakeLocks & Opportunistic Suspend\nStandard Linux systems idle by executing the `WFI` (Wait For Interrupt) instruction while keeping bus power rails and clock PLLs energized. Android's **Opportunistic Suspend** model transitions the entire SoC into **Deep Sleep (Suspend-to-RAM)** when the display turns off, cutting power rails to CPU cores and secondary peripherals. Background processes cannot prevent Deep Sleep unless holding an explicit kernel WakeLock (`/sys/power/wake_lock`).\n\n### 3.2 Energy-Aware Scheduling (EAS) and DynamIQ Multi-Core\nTraditional schedulers placed tasks on whichever core had the shortest queue (fair time-sharing). In heterogeneous architectures (big.LITTLE / DynamIQ), running a thread on a Cortex-X prime core consumes up to 15x more energy than on a Cortex-A500 efficiency core.\n\nEAS integrates:\n- **PELT (Per-Entity Load Tracking)**: Real-time tracking of individual thread CPU utilization.\n- **Energy Model (EM)**: Pre-calibrated tables mapping `(Core_Type, Frequency) -> Power_mW`.\n- **Placement Algorithm**: When waking a task, EAS simulates energy consumption across all core candidates and routes the task to the cluster that yields the lowest total system energy.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Summary & Verification Takeaways\nMastering Android power management requires understanding how kernel WakeLocks, EAS task placement, and DVFS OPP tables cooperate to maximize battery longevity while maintaining 60Hz/120Hz responsiveness.\n",
