    "title_eng": "5.3 Window Management Subsystem (WMS) & Input Event Routing",
    "content_eng": "# 5.3 WindowManagerService (WMS) & Input Event Pipeline: InputReader, InputDispatcher & ANR\n### Android Systems & Bionic Architecture Handbook — Module 5: Lecture 3\n\n**Course Series:** AOSF-HB-2026-SYS-MOD5-03  \n**Author:** Eunho Kim (@uno-km), AMEVA Systems Architecture & Research Engineering Group  \n**Target Architecture:** Linux evdev & Android InputManagerService (IMS)  \n**Curriculum Scope:** Kernel Input Subsystem, InputReader Thread, InputDispatcher, InputChannel Socket Pairs, ANR Governance  \n**Compliance Standard:** Apache-2.0 / OpenSSF Best Practices / CNCF Neutrality Guidelines  \n\n---\n\n## 1. Abstract & Input Event Chronology\n\nTouch responsiveness defines user perception of mobile system quality. When a user touches the capacitive digitizer, hardware interrupt requests (IRQ) trigger the Linux kernel touch driver, emitting raw input packets to `/dev/input/event*`.\n\nProcessing touch events and routing them across multi-window focus surfaces is managed by **InputManagerService (IMS)** and **WindowManagerService (WMS)** across two dedicated threads:\n1. **`InputReader` Thread**: Reads raw Linux `input_event` structs from `/dev/input/event*` using `epoll`, normalizes coordinates into display pixel spaces, and cooks raw touches into `MotionEvent` objects.\n2. **`InputDispatcher` Thread**: Identifies the currently focused window from WMS's z-order surface hierarchy and dispatches events through non-blocking **Unix Domain Socket pairs (`InputChannel`)**.\n\nIf an application fails to process an input event within **5,000 milliseconds**, the dispatcher triggers an **Application Not Responding (ANR)** dialog.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Interactive WASM Systems Engineering Lab (Colab-Style Cells)\n\n### [Cell 1~3] Python Input Event Dispatcher & ANR Timeout Simulator\n```python\n# [Colab Cell 1: Environment & Input Dispatcher Setup]\nimport sys\nimport time\n\nprint(\"=== [Cell 1: InputDispatcher & ANR Model Initialized] ===\")\nprint(\"Python Architecture:\", sys.version.split()[0])\n\n# [Colab Cell 2: Android Input Dispatcher & 5000ms ANR Timer]\nclass InputDispatcherSimulator:\n    def __init__(self, anr_threshold_ms=5000):\n        self.anr_threshold_ms = anr_threshold_ms\n\n    def dispatch_event(self, app_main_thread_blocked_ms):\n        start_time = time.perf_counter()\n        \n        # Simulate app thread response latency\n        if app_main_thread_blocked_ms > self.anr_threshold_ms:\n            return {\n                \"status\": \"ANR_TRIGGERED\",\n                \"blocked_duration_ms\": app_main_thread_blocked_ms,\n                \"action\": \"Dump traces.txt & Display ANR Dialog\"\n            }\n        else:\n            return {\n                \"status\": \"EVENT_CONSUMED\",\n                \"latency_ms\": app_main_thread_blocked_ms,\n                \"action\": \"Processed smoothly\"\n            }\n\n# [Colab Cell 3: Verification & Execution Benchmark]\nsim = InputDispatcherSimulator()\nres_fast = sim.dispatch_event(app_main_thread_blocked_ms=12)\nres_stalled = sim.dispatch_event(app_main_thread_blocked_ms=5200)\n\nprint(f\"\nScenario 1 (Normal Scroll) : Status={res_fast['status']}, Latency={res_fast['latency_ms']} ms\")\nprint(f\"Scenario 2 (Stalled Worker): Status={res_stalled['status']}, Action={res_stalled['action']}\")\n\nassert res_stalled[\"status\"] == \"ANR_TRIGGERED\", \"5200ms must trigger ANR\"\nprint(\"Status: 100% Deterministic ANR Boundary PASS\")\n```\n\n### [POSIX Shell] Input Event Nodes & Window Focus Probe\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -eu\necho \"=== [Android Input Subsystem & Event Node Probe] ===\"\necho \"Timestamp: $(date -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S UTC')\"\n\necho -e \"\n[1. Linux Input Event Devices]\"\nif [ -d \"/dev/input\" ]; then\n    ls -l /dev/input/event* 2>/dev/null || echo \"  No event nodes exposed\"\nelse\n    echo \"  [Sandbox Mode] Virtual Input: /dev/input/event0 (Touchscreen Digitizer)\"\nfi\n\necho -e \"\n[2. Input Channel Socket Pair Status]\"\necho \"  Socket Pair  : InputChannel (Sender: system_server, Receiver: app_process)\"\necho \"  Queue Bound  : Max 100 unhandled events before dispatch stall\"\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Input Event Dispatch Sequence & Channel Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant Touch as Touch Hardware / Kernel IRQ\n    participant Reader as InputReader Thread\n    participant Disp as InputDispatcher Thread\n    participant App as App Main Thread (Looper)\n\n    Touch->>Reader: Emits /dev/input/eventX (Linux input_event)\n    Reader->>Reader: Cook raw coordinates into MotionEvent\n    Reader->>Disp: Post cooked event to InboundQueue\n    Disp->>Disp: Query WMS for focused window & target InputChannel\n    Disp->>App: Send event via Unix Domain Socket (InputChannel)\n    Note over App: App Looper processes event on MainThread\n    App->>Disp: Send ConsumerFinishedSignal (Ack)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. ANR (Application Not Responding) Root Causes\n1. **Main Thread Blocking**: Executing synchronous network I/O, heavy disk reads, or database transactions on the main UI thread.\n2. **Deadlock in Binder IPC**: Application main thread waits on a synchronous Binder call to another service that is deadlocked or blocked.\n3. **CPU Starvation**: Heavy background threads consuming 100% CPU on big cores, starving the main thread's message queue.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Summary & Verification Takeaways\nReal-time mobile touch handling depends on dedicated kernel epoll readers and asynchronous socket pairs, backed by strict 5,000ms ANR deadlines to enforce application responsiveness.\n",
    "tags": "#WMS #WindowManager #InputSubsystem #InputFlinger #EventHub #TouchDispatch #ANRForensics",
